  { q: "Is 3 days enough for Lhasa?", a: "Yes. Three full days is the minimum to see the major sights while allowing for altitude acclimatization. Day 1 is for settling in and light exploration. Day 2 covers Potala Palace. Day 3 covers Jokhang Temple, Barkhor Street, and Sera Monastery. Add a fourth day if you want to include Drepung Monastery or Norbulingka." },
  { q: "Should I book tours or explore independently?", a: "Foreign visitors must be part of an organized tour to enter Tibet, but within Lhasa city limits, you can explore freely during your free time. Most tour packages include your guide for the major sights (Potala, Jokhang) and leave evenings free." },
  { q: "What is the best order to visit the main sights?", a: "Potala Palace first (morning, when energy is highest). Jokhang Temple second (morning or late afternoon). Sera Monastery in the afternoon to catch the monk debates. Barkhor Street is best in the early morning or evening when the light is beautiful and the crowds are thinner." },
  { q: "How much walking is involved?", a: "A lot. Potala Palace alone involves climbing the equivalent of 13 stories of stairs at altitude. Jokhang Temple and Barkhor Street are flat but involve hours on your feet. Budget 15,000 to 20,000 steps per day. Comfortable shoes are not optional." },
  { q: "Can I do a day trip from Lhasa?", a: "On a 3-day itinerary, do not plan day trips. Use all three days for Lhasa city. If you have 4 or 5 days, consider a day trip to Yamdrok Lake (spectacular turquoise alpine lake, 2 hours each way) or Ganden Monastery (1.5 hours east of Lhasa, less crowded than city monasteries)." },
];
